    "logo": null,
    "contact_source_ids": [],
    "description": "Sustainable product design considers environmental impacts alongside durability, repair, use, and eventual material recovery. Research involving Iranian universities shows how furniture design can be evaluated through life-cycle assessment, while leaving national adoption and verified commercial outcomes unresolved.",
    "tags": ["Sustainable design", "Ecodesign", "Life-cycle assessment", "Furniture", "Repairability", "Durability"],
    "status": null,
    "editorial_status": "ready_for_review",
    "as_of_date": "2026-10-26",
    "created_at": "2026-10-28T06:47:27Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-10-28T06:47:27Z",
    "seo": {
      "title": "Sustainable Product Design in Iran: Durability and Life Cycles",
      "description": "Understand sustainable product design through durability, repairability and furniture research involving Iranian universities."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "designing-the-life-cycle",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Designing beyond the point of sale",
        "content": "Sustainable product design asks how an object performs across its life, rather than judging it only by its appearance or initial material. The United Nations Economic Commission for Europe's terminology emphasizes durability, repairability, recyclability, and reduced environmental impact throughout the life cycle. These are connected objectives: making something easy to recycle does not, by itself, establish that it lasts or can be repaired. [s1]\n\nUNEP places design within a wider circular system involving producers, users, and other participants in the value chain. A designer can enable reuse or repair, but those opportunities also require arrangements that work after the product leaves the factory. This makes sustainable design relevant to service provision and material recovery as well as manufacturing. [s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "iranian-furniture-research",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Furniture as an Iranian research example",
        "content": "A study published in 2024, with authors affiliated with Iranian universities and Pennsylvania State University, combined life-cycle assessment with the analytic hierarchy process to examine furniture manufacturing. Life-cycle assessment evaluates environmental burdens within a specified system; the analytic hierarchy process organizes comparisons among decision criteria. Combining them connects environmental modeling with choices about production and materials. [s3]\n\nThe article's abstract identifies wood quality, supply management, connections, workforce training, and process design among important considerations. Its modeled alternatives included designing recyclable furniture, changing an imported wood input, and using recycled wood. The abstract discusses different environmental categories, including water toxicity, emissions, and land use. It therefore illustrates why a material substitution should be assessed against several outcomes rather than reduced to a single green label. [s3]\n\nThis is evidence of a research approach involving Iranian institutions. It does not establish that the alternatives were adopted across Iranian factories, that particular commercial products achieved the modeled results, or that one material is universally preferable. Those conclusions would require evidence beyond the inspected abstract. [s3]",
        "source_ids": ["s3"]
      },
      {
        "id": "durability-and-evidence",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Durability needs a clear definition",
        "content": "A scholarly review of product design and durability connects longer product life with reuse, repair, refurbishment, and remanufacturing. It also identifies a need for better design methods and assessment tools. Durability concerns the ability to continue performing under defined conditions; it cannot be inferred solely from a product's weight, price, or recycled content. The review provides an international conceptual framework, rather than evidence of Iranian market performance. [s4]\n\nFor readers assessing an Iranian product or design proposal, these sources suggest useful questions: what function is being maintained, which life-cycle stages were assessed, and how will repair or recovery occur? These are questions for interpreting evidence, not a certification checklist. A credible account should identify the comparison being made and distinguish a modeled design option from a demonstrated product outcome. [s1][s3][s4]\n\nThe Iranian furniture example shows the value of evaluating design before waste occurs. The next evidentiary step is to connect such research with documented manufacturing decisions and measured performance in use. [s2][s3]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2", "s3", "s4"]
      }
    ],
